-### Parameters and Edge Cases
-*   **Threshold $e_\tau$:** Used to filter out incorrect depth estimates. Value not explicitly given in sources [S1].
-*   **Specular Regions:** In specular regions, reprojected patch intensities may not match, causing masks to be zero. This results in fewer supervised pixels but avoids supervision with incorrect depth estimates [S1, S2].
-*   **Single-View Visible Regions:** Transparent floaters may persist in regions visible in only one training view because reliable depth supervision is not available there [S2].
-*   **Depth Variance:** The depth supervision loss is imposed only on the mean depth, not on the variance [S2].
-*   **Base Model Performance:** The method is limited to cases where the base model achieves reasonable performance but suffers from distortions; it may not help if the base model performance is very poor [S2].
+### Parameters
+-   $e_\tau$: Threshold for reprojection MSE to filter out incorrect depth estimates. Value: Not explicitly given in sources, but used to mark depth reliable if MSE $< e_\tau$ [S1].
+-   $l_p, l_v$: Highest positional encoding frequencies for position and view. Typical values for NeRF are 10 and 4 respectively [S1, S2].
+-   $l_{ps}$: Reduced positional encoding frequency for smoothing augmentation coarse MLP. Value: Not explicitly given, but $l_{ps} < l_p$ [S2].
+
+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+-   **Specular Regions**: Reprojected patch intensities may not match, causing masks to be zero. This results in fewer supervised pixels but avoids supervision with incorrect depth estimates [S1, S2].
+-   **Single View Visibility**: In regions visible in only one training view, reliable depth supervision is not available, leading to transparent floaters [S2].
+-   **Poor Base Performance**: The method is limited to cases where the base model achieves reasonable performance but suffers from distortions; it may not help if the base model performance is very poor [S2].
+-   **Depth Variance**: The depth supervision loss is imposed only on the mean depth, not on the variance. Experiments with minimizing standard deviation led to stronger artifacts [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Simple-NeRF queries the coarse MLPs 192 times and fine MLPs 192 times (384 total queries per pixel), whereas standard NeRF queries coarse MLPs 64 times and fine MLPs 192 times (256 total) [S2]. Training takes approximately 1.5 times more time than standard NeRF due to the augmentations on the coarse NeRF [S2]. Disabling the coarse-fine consistency loss ($L_{cfc}$) increases training time by almost 2x because augmentations must be included for the fine NeRF as well [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Performance:**
-    *   **LLFF (2 views):** SSIM 0.6501, PSNR 19.57, LPIPS 0.2688. Outperforms RegNeRF (SSIM 0.5712, PSNR 18.52, LPIPS 0.3056) and FreeNeRF (SSIM 0.6322, PSNR 19.52, LPIPS 0.2754) [S1].
-    *   **LLFF (4 views):** SSIM 0.7016, PSNR 21.99, LPIPS 0.2633. Outperforms RegNeRF (SSIM 0.6645, PSNR 21.32, LPIPS 0.2794) and FreeNeRF (SSIM 0.6764, PSNR 21.91, LPIPS 0.2848) [S1].
-    *   **RealEstate-10K (2 views):** MAE 0.3269, SROCC 0.9215. Outperforms DS-NeRF (MAE 0.7164, SROCC 0.6660) [S2].
-    *   **NeRF-LLFF (2 views):** MAE 0.1420, SROCC 0.8480. Outperforms DS-NeRF (MAE 0.2074, SROCC 0.7230) [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Mitigates floaters and shape-radiance ambiguity without pre-training. Effective in sparse view settings.
-*   **Limitations:** Increased training time and compute overhead. Less effective if the base model is very poor. Transparent floaters may persist in single-view visible regions.
+-   **Time Complexity**: Simple-NeRF queries coarse MLPs 192 times and fine MLPs 192 times (384 total), whereas standard NeRF queries coarse MLPs 64 times and fine MLPs 192 times (256 total) [S2]. Training takes approximately 1.5 times more time than standard NeRF [S2]. Disabling $L_{cfc}$ increases training time by almost 2x due to the inclusion of augmentations for the fine NeRF [S2].
+-   **Memory Complexity**: Training augmented models adds compute and memory overhead during training [S2].
+-   **Performance**:
+    -   On LLFF dataset with 2 views: SSIM 0.6501, PSNR 19.57, LPIPS 0.2688 [S1].
+    -   On LLFF dataset with 4 views: SSIM 0.7016, PSNR 21.99, LPIPS 0.2633 [S1].
+    -   On RealEstate-10K with 2 views: MAE 0.3269, SROCC 0.9215 [S2].
+    -   On NeRF-LLFF with 2 views: MAE 0.1420, SROCC 0.8480 [S2].
+-   **Strengths**: Mitigates floaters and incorrect depth issues simultaneously, unlike RegNeRF which fuses objects at different depths [S2]. Does not require pre-training on large datasets [S1].
+-   **Limitations**: Limited to sparse view scenarios where base model is reasonably good. Transparent floaters persist in single-view visible regions [S2].
 
 ## Background
-Simple-NeRF was introduced by Nagabhushan Somraj et al. in 2023 [S1]. It was later expanded as part of the Simple-RF family in 2024 [S2]. The method is designed for few-shot/sparse input NeRF training on datasets like LLFF, using DS-NeRF as the baseline architecture [S1]. It addresses the limitations of prior works that require pre-training on large datasets (e.g., PixelNeRF, IBRNet) or external depth models (e.g., DDP-NeRF, SCADE) [S1]. The approach of using augmented models in tandem with the main radiance field model is closest to the Dual-Student architecture [Ke et al. 2019], but uses complementary abilities rather than identical models [S1, S2].
+Simple-NeRF was introduced by Nagabhushan Somraj et al. in 2023 [S1, S2]. It is part of the Simple Radiance Fields (Simple-RF) family, which also includes Simple-TensoRF and Simple-ZipNeRF [S2]. The method is designed for few-shot/sparse input NeRF training, using DS-NeRF as the baseline architecture [S1]. It views the framework as semi-supervised learning, where sparse depth from SfM provides limited labels and remaining pixels are unlabeled data [S1, S2].
 
